        "title":  "Build a multi-account AI agent with AgentCore Gateway and MCP",
        "source":  "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "url":  "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-a-multi-account-ai-agent-with-agentcore-gateway-and-mcp/",
        "date":  "2026-09-24",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "This AWS technical guide demonstrates how to architect a distributed AI system where a centralized agent in an AWS platform account coordinates with data-owning line-of-business accounts through Model Context Protocol servers. The solution uses Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway to aggregate tools across accounts, with OAuth 2.0 machine-to-machine authentication and Cedar-based policy enforcement for security. Each line-of-business account maintains data sovereignty by exposing only query results rather than raw datasets.",
        "execSummary":  "This architecture pattern solves one of enterprise multi-team AI governance\u0027s hardest problems: how to give a centralized AI agent cross-organizational tool access without creating a data lake everyone can query. The Cedar-based policy enforcement and OAuth M2M authentication model maps directly onto existing enterprise IAM patterns, lowering the organizational adoption barrier compared to bespoke agent security designs. For enterprise architects evaluating agent orchestration, AgentCore Gateway\u0027s MCP aggregation approach offers a vendor-managed alternative to building custom tool registries—but it creates AWS platform dependency that should be weighed against portability requirements. The data sovereignty preservation pattern (returning results, not raw data) is a reusable design principle applicable beyond AWS.",
        "vendorLean":  "Leans AWS — AWS technical blog promoting Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway and MCP services.",
        "imageUrl":  "https://d2908q01vomqb2.cloudfront.net/f1f836cb4ea6efb2a0b1b99f41ad8b103eff4b59/2026/09/21/ML-20091-1.png",
        "isNew":  false
