        "source":  "NVIDIA AI Blog",
        "url":  "https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ai-security-agent-stack/",
        "date":  "2026-09-21",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "The article argues that AI security requires systematic engineering practices across all layers of an agent stack, not just model-level guardrails. Key recommendations include enforceable controls at every layer, clear ownership and accountability, and continuous pre- and post-deployment security testing. Organizations are urged to collaborate openly on defensive strategies so that shared lessons strengthen enterprise defenses faster than attackers can exploit them.",
        "execSummary":  "Enterprises deploying AI agents face a compounding security risk if they rely on agent reasoning alone to enforce boundaries rather than building enforceable controls into infrastructure, tooling, and data access layers. The article identifies named ownership, documented least-privilege policies, and mandatory human approval for permission changes as non-negotiable governance requirements before scaling agentic deployments. Open sharing of successful security patterns—as demonstrated by CrowdStrike and Capital One—accelerates collective defense and sets an emerging industry standard. Customer-support and enterprise operations leaders should treat agent security architecture as a prerequisite to production rollout, not a post-launch audit item.",
        "vendorLean":  "Leans NVIDIA — promotes NVIDIA\u0027s perspective on agent security best practices and the broader NVIDIA agent ecosystem.",
